#{[17592186605113 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(9{x}^{9}) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(9\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $9$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9} &=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(9{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186605134 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }(3{u}^{3}-u)^{5} & =\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }(3{u}^{3})^{5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }243{u}^{15} \\\\  & =243\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }{u}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605139 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{4}+5x}{8{x}^{5}-10} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(8-\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{8-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186605142 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6{x}^{9}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{9}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $6$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{9} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{9}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6{x}^{9})=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186605143 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4-{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4-{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{5}^{4-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{5}^{4-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty$\n\n![](images/df/dfacdebf754f239c4b5feebed75b49d1d9061594593286b7b837799372076cae.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{5}^{4-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605147 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }{(4{z}^{3}-3)}^{3} & =\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }(4{z}^{3})^{3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }(64{z}^{9}) \\\\  & =64\\cdot {lim}_{z\\to -\\infty }({z}^{9}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605858 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether  the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{6n-2}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{6n-2}{3n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6n}{n}-\\frac{2}{n})}{\\left(\\frac{3n}{n}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6-\\frac{2}{n})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $2.$"] [17592186605904 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{7-2{n}^{6}}{3{n}^{6}+5{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{6},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7-2{n}^{6}}{3{n}^{6}+5{n}^{3}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{2{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}}+\\frac{5{n}^{3}}{{n}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{n}^{6}}-2)}{3+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{3+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [17592186605906 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I, the limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7}{{n}^{7}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{(3+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(1+\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence III diverges."] [17592186605908 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}+3n}{n-{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{n}^{2}+3n}{n-{n}^{5}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{5}}+\\frac{3n}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}})}{\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186608193 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{5}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{5}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|5x| \\\\  & =5|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186608200 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{n+1}}{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x}{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=2.$"] [17592186608203 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.  \n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-3,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2+}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}(\\sqrt[3]{x-1})=\\sqrt[3]{2-1}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to 2-}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x-1))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (2-1))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-3,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,1].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x-1))$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186609836 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\frac{2n}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{2n}{3}\\right)=-\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$ is also divergent."] [17592186609860 :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1+2}{4}=\\frac{3}{4},\n$$\n\nIts $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{n+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{n+2}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(5+n)}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(5+n)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent."] [17592186609861 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =5+5=5\\cdot 2 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =5\\cdot 2+5=5\\cdot 3 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =5\\cdot 3+5=5\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $1\\le n\\le 20$ is ${s}_{n}=5n.$  In particular, ${s}_{20}=5\\cdot 20=100.$\n\nLet's compute the first few partial sums for $n\\ge 21:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{21} & =100+0=100 \\\\ {s}_{22} & =100+0=100 \\\\ {s}_{23} & =100+0=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 21$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=100.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is convergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }100=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converges to $100.$"] [17592186609862 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{3}n)=\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{3}\n$$ is also divergent."] [17592186610950 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt[3]{2-3x} & =\\sqrt[3]{2-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186611600 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1}} & =\\frac{6(0)}{\\sqrt{9(0)+1}-\\sqrt{6(0)+1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1})}{(\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1})(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1})}{(\\sqrt{9x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6x+1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1})}{(9x+1)-(6x+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1})}{3x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2(\\sqrt{9x+1}+\\sqrt{6x+1}) \\\\  & =2(1+1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186611630 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)}{2} & =\\frac{arcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186611648 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1/2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613214 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1} & =(-1)\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613738 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{6}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{6}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{x}}=\\infty$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/be/be96cbb3df39d061f6ec905b676b91d38dda2083c4eac032a930df7b8754148c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{6}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613741 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x/2}]}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{4/2}-{e}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x/2}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x/2}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x/2}=\\infty$\n\n![](images/af/af890a97e5ece083e14e4a453d736ba970191a69df8ce521e7179feb771eb03f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{2}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613774 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}9{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[2{e}^{x/2}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(2{e}^{-1}-2{e}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2}{e}-2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x/2}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x/2}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x/2}=\\infty$\n\n![](images/7c/7c91744afa42f2db85f21805cf8c19f441f24fc5c9d79bf0f7242745fb825f4e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}9{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2}{e}-2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{2}{e}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613786 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{t+1}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a+1}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(9-z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{(9-z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{9-z}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{9-a}-\\frac{1}{9-10}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{9-a}+1) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and III are divergent."] [17592186613800 :question/worked-solution "The marginal density function for $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613814 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{5}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{5}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-2x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{2a}}-\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{2x}}=\\infty$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/6e/6ed2b3d89b91381f6523c4e8e13fbeb7645468846956949e4b1d9923bb4308df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{5}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{2a}}-\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186615373 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{y(x)-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{y(x)-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[y(x)-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(x)}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(0)}{{sec}^{2}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{0}{1}\\right)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186616939 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =24\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =24\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z\\,{|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =24\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & =24\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{z\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1e6e2280faa5a723d5c2370d4e3c851beeca324dd648f33aca4658393748a7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =24\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186616976 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}\\frac{20}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]}_{a}^{1/5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/da/daa942ec89fba177dfa505e127f0471e1c6309d909c6312b1694ccf898750879.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}\\frac{20}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186616982 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{5}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{6}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/ee/eef6460d2f20f1f1a4fb1390411d9a4e97f7e8025d432428f2c51d4777cc11c2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186616985 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={5}^{t}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}={5}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}u={5}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={5}^{t}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{t}}{1+{5}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{t}}{1+{5}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{t}}{1+{5}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617010 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186618734 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡a-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡a$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡a) \\\\  & =-1+\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡a$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."] [17592186618747 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡a-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡a$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡a) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡a$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."] [17592186618808 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a) \\\\  & =-2\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186618814 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({a}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186619758 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=f(3)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(f(x)-f(3)) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)-f(3) \\\\  & =-2-(-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186619781 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186619786 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/cb/cb1b3c7abc2aa0d437e83e1b5c4837ce1b71f232dd2e618cc7f7fa5ea3d588c8.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty\n$$"] [17592186619814 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186620817 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+4}{3x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622760 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}=\\infty$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/75/7594807e690df03df5fd7cd80221edd8a0b6ddfc5e89d5e17640abf8dd66ed94.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622768 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {3}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{2u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {3}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{2x}}=\\infty$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/6a/6a3d79553fbd199df26b091c355c0b00221ef3215189d603e925b2b61a569f2b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {3}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622777 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{1/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{1/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{u}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty$\n\n![](images/3d/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{1/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186628493 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$"] [17592186628493 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=-(0)^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0=f(0).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [17592186629292 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{6x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{6x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186629296 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|-1} & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x-1}\\,{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{a-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand ${e}^{-|x|-1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186629312 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=6x-3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{67}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{67/6}\\frac{1}{(6x-3)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{6{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{4}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{67/6}\\frac{1}{(6x-3)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=4.\n$$"] [17592186629324 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={5}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={5}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+{5}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186630260 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=16.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\sqrt{4f(x)} &=\\sqrt{4\\cdot 16}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634587 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-2n/3} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634644 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi x})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{\\pi x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186634665 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{4} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186639172 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{3}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{3}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(h)={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Now,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & ={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality and the triangle inequality, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖f(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h|}{‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{-2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{-2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{‖h{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639173 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$, $g(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}3 \\\\  & =3\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$, $g(x)={x}^{3}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639174 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{4}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{4}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h{‖}^{3} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(h)={h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖ \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{-1}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{-1}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{‖h{‖}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639187 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ means  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $g(x)=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639197 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(\\mathbf{h}) = o(\\mathbf{h})$ as $\\mathbf{h}\\to \\mathbf{a}$ means that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{h} \\to \\mathbf{a}} \\dfrac{f(\\mathbf{h})}{\\|\\mathbf{h}\\|} = 0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $\\mathbf{h} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ within some neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(\\mathbf{h}) = \\|\\mathbf{h}\\|^{3/2}$ and $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{3/2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h{‖}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\sqrt{‖h‖} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(\\mathbf{h}) = \\dfrac{h_1^2+h_2^2}2$ and $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}{2}\\right)}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖ \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(\\mathbf{h}) = \\sqrt{\\|\\mathbf{h}\\|}$ and $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{‖h‖}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{1/2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{‖h{‖}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{‖h‖}} \\\\  & =\\infty  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639201 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ means \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $g(x)=x.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{-2}$ and $g(x)=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639202 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x) = o(g(x))$ means \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x) > 0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $f(x) = e^{-x}$ and $g(x) = 1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $e^{-x} = o(1)$ is a true statement."] [17592186639207 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ means  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=5$ and $g(x)=x.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{1}{x}$ and $g(x)=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I, II, and III are true."] [17592186639208 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x,$ $g(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2x+1} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}x+1 \\\\  & =1\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{3}+x$, $g(x)={x}^{4}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639209 :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\nf(x) = o(g(x)), \\qquad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a}\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x) > 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nIn our case, we have $f(x) = \\sin x,$ $g(x) = 1,$ and $a = 0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin{x} = o(1)$ is a true statement."] [17592186639210 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=3({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{3({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{3‖h{‖}^{2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}3‖h‖ \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=\\sqrt{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=4‖h‖$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{4‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}4 \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639216 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})$, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\cdot 2(0)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=2.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}(x-2) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{-x}$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639229 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{x}$, $g(x)={x}^{-1}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}x{e}^{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{-1}$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639235 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ means  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{-1}$ and $g(x)=x.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)={x}^{-1}.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $g(x)=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186639243 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ means  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ and $g(x)={x}^{-1}.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=1$ and $g(x)=x.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=x.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{x} \\\\  & =1\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186639250 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{x}$, $g(x)=x-1$ and $a=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{e}{x-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=5$, $g(x)=x-1$ and $a=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5}{x-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{5}$, $g(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186639252 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}‖h‖ \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h‖$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(h)={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Now,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & ={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality  and the triangle inequality, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖f(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h|}{‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186639256 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+1)\\, & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186639266 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=5({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})^{-1}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{5({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})^{-1}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{5‖h{‖}^{-2}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{5}{‖h{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{4}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{4}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h{‖}^{3} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=‖h{‖}^{-3}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖h{‖}^{-3}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{‖h{‖}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\infty \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186639309 :tutorial/content "Little-o notation can also be used for multivariable functions. Let $f$ be a function of several variables defined in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$ We say that $f(\\mathbf h) = o(\\mathbf h)$ as $\\mathbf h \\to \\mathbf a$ if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{h} \\to \\mathbf a} \\dfrac{f(\\mathbf h)}{\\|\\mathbf h\\|} =0.\n$$\n\nFor instance, let $f(x,y) = x^2.$ Then $f(\\mathbf h) = o(\\mathbf h)$ as $\\mathbf h \\to \\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTo show this, first let $\\mathbf h = h_1\\, \\mathbf i + h_2\\, \\mathbf{j} .$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & ={h}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i+0\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =|{h}_{1}|\\cdot ‖h‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $h_1 \\to 0$ as $\\mathbf h\\to \\mathbf{0},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖f(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i)\\cdot h|}{‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|{h}_{1}|\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}|{h}_{1}| \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186639310 :tutorial/content "We can also use little-o notation to compare the behavior of two functions at a point.\n\nSuppose that $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions defined on an open interval containing the point $x=a.$ Then we say that $f = o(g), \\, x \\to a,$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x) > 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nFor instance, we can write \n\n$$\nx^2 = o(x), \\qquad x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}x \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, \n\n$$\nx \\neq o(x^3), \\qquad x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: If we do not mention what $x$ approaches, then it is considered to approach infinity. For example, if we say that $x = o(x^2),$ then we really mean $x = o(x^2), \\, x \\to \\infty.$"] [17592186639311 :tutorial/content "Consider two functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ We say that the function $f(x)$ is $o(g(x)),$ and write $f= o(g),$   if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x) > 0$ for sufficiently large $x.$ \n\nNote that:\n\n- The expression $f=o(g)$ reads as \"$f$ is **little-oh** of $g.$\"\n\n- The expression $f=o(g)$ can also be read as \"$f(x)$ is *much smaller than* $g(x)$ (for sufficiently large $x$).\"\n\nFor instance, we can write $x = o(x^2)$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $e^x \\neq o(x).$ By applying L'Hôpital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\infty  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186641601 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =2((x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2})-2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+2({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})-2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+2(2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})-2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2}+2({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(4x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2})+2({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(4x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+2({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (4x\\,i+4y\\,j)+2‖h{‖}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $2‖h{‖}^{2}=o(h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,2‖h{‖}^{2}\\,}{||h||} &=2\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (4x\\,i+4y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=4x\\,i+4y\\,j.\n$$"] [17592186641604 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true:\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+2(y+{h}_{2})^{2}-({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+2({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})-({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y{h}_{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+4y{h}_{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2})+{h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(2x{h}_{1}+4y{h}_{2})+{h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =h\\cdot (2x\\,i+4y\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe wish to show that $g(h)={h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2}=o(h).$ Indeed, \nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(h) & ={h}_{1}^{2}+2{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i+2{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot ({h}_{1}i+{h}_{2}j) \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i+2{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the Cauchy-Schwarz and triangle inequalities, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i+2{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i+2{h}_{2}\\,i‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖+2‖{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+2|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖g(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i+2{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h|}{‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(|{h}_{1}|+2|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(|{h}_{1}|+2|{h}_{2}|) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nsince ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (2x\\,i+4y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\n$$"] [17592186641608 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true:  \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x+{h}_{1})^{2}-(y+{h}_{2})^{2}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})-({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x{h}_{1}-2y{h}_{2})+{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x{h}_{1}-2y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(2x{h}_{1}-2y{h}_{2})+{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =h\\cdot (2x\\,i-2y\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to show that $g(h)={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}=o(h).$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(h) & ={h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot ({h}_{1}i+{h}_{2}j) \\\\  & =({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwarz and triangle inequalities, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖g(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,i)\\cdot h|}{‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nsince ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (2x\\,i-2y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\n$$"] [17592186641621 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $\\nabla f$ such that the following equality is true:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x+\\mathbf h) - f(\\mathbf x)= \\mathbf h\\cdot  \\nabla f(\\mathbf x) + o(\\mathbf h)\n$$\n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $\\mathbf h = h_1\\,\\mathbf i + h_2\\,\\mathbf j,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\  & =2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2xh_1 + 2yh_2$ is linear with respect to $h_1$ and $h_2,$ we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(2x{h}_{1}+2y{h}_{2})+({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =h\\cdot (2x\\,i+2y\\,j)+{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =h\\cdot (2x\\,i+2y\\,j)+||h|{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $||\\mathbf h||^2 = o(\\mathbf h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{||h|{|}^{2}}{||h||} &=\\lim_{h\\to 0}||h||=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x + \\mathbf h) - f(\\mathbf x)  = \\mathbf h\\cdot \\underbrace{(2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j)}_{\\nabla f} + o(\\mathbf h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $\\nabla f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = 2x\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2y\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186641638 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(\\frac{1}{2}(\\,(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2})+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\,+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4})+x{h}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{1}^{2}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{2}^{2}-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}=o(h),$ because \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\,}{||h||} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=x\\,i+y\\,j.\n$$"] [17592186641655 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true:\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =2(x+{h}_{1})-(y+{h}_{2})-(2x-y) \\\\  & =(2x-y)+(2{h}_{1}-{h}_{2})-(2x-y) \\\\  & =2{h}_{1}-{h}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{h}_{1}-{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =2{h}_{1}-{h}_{2} \\\\  & =(2\\,i-j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (2\\,i-j)+0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $0=o(h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{0}{‖h‖} &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (2\\,i-j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=2\\,i-j.\n$$"] [17592186641663 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =\\frac{(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[{x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}-\\cancel{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\sqrt{2}(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and  ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =\\sqrt{2}(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =h\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(x\\,i+y\\,j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, notice that $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}=o(h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\,}{||h||} &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(x\\,i+y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally,  by comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=\\sqrt{2}(x\\,i+y\\,j).\n$$"] [17592186641675 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true:\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =((x+{h}_{1})+2)-(x+2) \\\\  & =x+{h}_{1}+2-x-2 \\\\  & ={h}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${h}_{1}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & ={h}_{1} \\\\  & =(i+0\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =h\\cdot i+0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $0=o(h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{0}{‖h‖}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot i+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=i.\n$$"] [17592186641743 :tutorial/content "For multivariable functions, *differentiability implies continuity*, just like for single-variable functions. Here, we will prove this result.\n\nSuppose that $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a multivariable function that is differentiable at the point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a+h)-f(a) &=∇f(a)\\cdot h+o(h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the triangle inequality followed by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(a+h)-f(a)| & =|∇f(a)\\cdot h+o(h)| \\\\  & \\le |∇f(a)\\cdot h|+|o(h)| \\\\  & \\le ‖∇f(a)‖\\cdot ‖h‖+|o(h)|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the limit as $\\mathbf h\\to\\mathbf 0,$ we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}|f(a+h)-f(a)| & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}‖∇f(a)‖\\cdot ‖h‖+\\lim_{h\\to 0}|o(h)| \\\\  & =‖∇f(a)‖\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}‖h‖+\\lim_{h\\to 0}|o(h)| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf h \\to \\mathbf 0}|f(\\mathbf a+ \\mathbf h)-f(\\mathbf a) |=0,\n$$\n\n \nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf h \\to \\mathbf 0}f(\\mathbf a+ \\mathbf h) = f(\\mathbf a) ,\n$$\n\nwhich means that $f$ is continuous at $\\mathbf a$. \n\n**Watch out!** Although differentiability implies continuity, the converse of this statement is *not* true in general. Continuity does *not* imply differentiability. A function can be continuous at a point but not differentiable at that point. This is true for both single-variable and multivariable functions."] [17592186641744 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=a$ with derivative $f'(a)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nWe cannot use this definition straight off the bat to define the derivative of a multivariable function. The reason is that the inputs $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf h$ would both need to be vectors, yielding\n\n$$\nf'(\\mathbf a) = \\lim_{\\mathbf h\\to \\mathbf 0}\\dfrac {f(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf h) - f(\\mathbf a)} {\\mathbf h},\n$$\n\nand we have a problem because division by a vector is not defined!\n\nHowever, it is possible to use the single-variable definition and apply it to multivariable functions. We just need to manipulate the above definition a little. First, we rewrite the limit above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\right)-{f}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\right)-\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h{f}^{′}(a)}{h}\\right) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two limits using the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a) - hf'(a)} {h}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing \"little-oh\" notation, we can write the limit above as\n\n$$\nf(a+h) - f(a) - hf'(a) = o(h), \\qquad  h\\to 0\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\nf(a+h) - f(a) = hf'(a) + o(h), \\qquad h\\to 0.\n$$"] [17592186650331 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{5}{3{x}^{4}}\\right) & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{5}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186652926 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true:\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x+{h}_{1})+(y+{h}_{2})-(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+{h}_{1}+{h}_{2}-x-y \\\\  & ={h}_{1}+{h}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${h}_{1}+{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & ={h}_{1}+{h}_{2} \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (i+j)+0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $0=o(h),$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{0}{‖h‖}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (i+j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=i+j.\n$$"] [17592186652971 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =[4(y+{h}_{2})-(x+{h}_{1})+1]-[4y-x+1] \\\\  & =(4y-x+1)+(4{h}_{2}-{h}_{1})-(4y-x+1) \\\\  & =4{h}_{2}-{h}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{h}_{2}-{h}_{1}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =-{h}_{1}+4{h}_{2} \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (-i+4\\,j)+0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that   $0=o(h),$ because  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{0}{‖h‖} &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (-i+4\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=-i+4\\,j.\n$$"] [17592186653527 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$, $g(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=1$, $g(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=x$, $g(x)={x}^{-1}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186653530 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, $g(x)={x}^{-1}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({x}^{-1})^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-x) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=3$, $g(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=2.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(x)={2}^{x}-2$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{2}^{x}-2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{1}-2}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  only statements I and III are true."] [17592186653579 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)={h}_{1}{h}_{2}-{h}_{1}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Now,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & ={h}_{1}{h}_{2}-{h}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{1}\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-{h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{1}\\,j)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality and the triangle inequality, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(-{h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{1}\\,j)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖-{h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖-{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖{h}_{1}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{1}|)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(2|{h}_{1}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|f(h)|}{‖h‖} & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(2|{h}_{1}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(2|{h}_{1}|) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=-2\\sqrt{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{-2‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}-2 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=2{h}_{1}^{2}+3{h}_{2}^{2}$ and $a=0.$ Now,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & =2{h}_{1}^{2}+3{h}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{h}_{1}\\,i+3{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(2{h}_{1}\\,i+3{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality  and the triangle inequaly, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(2{h}_{1}\\,i+3{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖2{h}_{1}\\,i+3{h}_{2}\\,j‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖2{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖3{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(2|{h}_{1}|+3|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|f(h)|}{‖h‖} & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(2|{h}_{1}|+3|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(2|{h}_{1}|+3|{h}_{2}|) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186653582 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $g(x)>0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{x}^{2})$, $g(x)=x$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit applying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{x}^{2}))^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{x}^{2})\\cdot (1+2x)}{1} \\\\  & =1\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, we have $f(x)=5x$, $g(x)=x-1$ and $a=1.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5x}{x-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{2}$, $g(x)={x}^{5}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  none of the statements are true."] [17592186653584 :question/worked-solution "The notation $f(h)=o(h)$ as $h\\to a$ means that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖}=0,\n$$\n\nprovided that $h\\ne 0$ within some neighborhood of $a.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=\\sqrt{‖h‖({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{‖h‖({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{‖h‖}\\sqrt{{h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{‖h‖}‖h‖}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\sqrt{‖h‖} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $f(h)=\\frac{{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}}{2}$ and $a=0.$ Now,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & =\\frac{{h}_{1}^{2}-{h}_{2}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality  and the triangle inequality, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h| & \\le ‖{h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j‖\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & \\le (‖{h}_{1}\\,i‖+‖{h}_{2}\\,j‖)\\cdot ‖h‖ \\\\  & =(|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${h}_{1}\\to 0,\\,{h}_{2}\\to 0$ as $h\\to 0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{‖f(h)‖}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|({h}_{1}\\,i-{h}_{2}\\,j)\\cdot h|}{2‖h‖} \\\\  & \\le {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{((|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|)\\cdot ‖h‖)}{2‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{|{h}_{1}|+|{h}_{2}|}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $f(h)=\\sqrt[3]{‖h‖}$ and $a=0.$ Computing the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to a}\\frac{f(h)}{‖h‖} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{‖h‖}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,\\,\\,{‖h‖}^{1/3}}{‖h‖} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{‖h{‖}^{2/3}} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186654751 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n{x}^{3}/3} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654792 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/y} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/y} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/y} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656248 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656248 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, we have $x>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
